<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Ten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1310">1310</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Consiglio dei Dieci, The Ten</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of Ten was one of the major governing bodies of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1310 to 1797, created by Doge Pietro Gradenigo as a temporary body to investigate the plot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajamonte-tiepolo">Bajamonte Tiepolo</a> and Marco Querini and made permanent in 1455. Its ten patrician members, elected by the Great Council for one year, held broad powers over state security and could punish nobles with banishment or death; on its orders Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marino-faliero">Marino Faliero</a> was executed in 1355. With secret funds, anonymous informers and police powers, the Ten and the Collegio formed the inner circle of patricians who in effect ruled the republic, and by the end of the 16th century the Ten oversaw its large intelligence network.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-ten.jpg" alt="Council of Ten" width="330" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Hayez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Hayez_010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Senate directed foreign policy, trade and war; the Collegio prepared business; and the <b>Council of Ten</b>, set up after the conspiracy of Bajamonte Tiepolo in 1310, guarded state security with secret procedures.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Council elected almost all magistrates, among them a Senate of about 300 that handled finance, trade and foreign affairs, the Council of Forty, the highest court, and the <b>Council of Ten</b>, a security tribunal with secret procedures.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Most ruled until death, but a few were forced out, and in 1355 the <b>Council of Ten</b> condemned Doge Marino Faliero to death for trying to make himself lord of Venice.</blockquote><a href="/societies/venice">Social Hierarchy of the Republic of Venice</a></li></ul></section>
